            <ArticleTitle>The Developmental History of IgE and IgG4 Antibodies in Relation to Atopy, Eosinophilic Esophagitis, and the Modified TH2 Response.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>45</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11882-016-0621-x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A common reaction from anyone confronted with allergy is the question: what prevents universal allergy? We will discuss recent findings in the mouse system that have provided us with clues on why allergy is not more common. We will also address one crucial aspect of atopic allergy in humans, which is absent in most mouse model systems, an IgG/IgE ratio &lt;10. We consider the typical mouse IgE response to be more closely related to the &quot;modified TH2&quot; response in humans. We will discuss the similarities and differences between the IgE and IgG4 response to allergens and an update on the IgG4 B cell, partly derived from studies on eosinophilic esophagitis and IgG4-related diseases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
